{"type":"message","id":"bcaf108c","parentId":"41832dc9","timestamp":"2026-05-18T11:09:58.654Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_a36Dmd4tRHDaUE8kGnIPWhp2","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# COINOGRAPHY EDITORIAL TEMPLATE\n\nThis document is your absolute source of truth. You MUST write the article EXACTLY following the numbered sections below. Deviation is strictly prohibited.\n\n## 🔴 CRITICAL WRITING RULES (READ FIRST)\n- **NO EM-DASHES:** Never use the `—` character anywhere in the article. Replace with a comma, a period, or restructure the sentence.\n- **NATURAL WRITING:** Never use AI phrases: \"it's worth noting\", \"it is important to note\", \"delve into\", \"in conclusion\", \"furthermore\", \"moreover\", \"in summary\", \"the crypto landscape\", \"the world of crypto\", \"a testament to\", \"shed light on\".\n- **TONE:** Direct. Trader-focused. Analytical. No hype, no fluff, no adjectives like \"amazing\" or \"shocking.\"\n- **PARAGRAPHS:** 1–4 sentences max. Break aggressively. One idea per paragraph.\n- **BULLET LISTS:** 3–4 items only. Used inside H3 sections only. Never in the conclusion or opening.\n- **ANCHOR LINKS:** You MUST naturally embed 2-3 markdown links inside the body text. These links MUST point to the exact source URLs provided in your RESEARCH_JSON. Example: `[according to recent data from Decrypt](https://decrypt.co/...)`\n- **CHART SOURCES:** The orchestrator will provide the charts. Simply embed exactly 2 mentions of the `[CHART_PLACEHOLDER]` tag inside the H2 sections.\n- **KEYWORD PLACEMENT:** \n  - Primary keyword in H1 (first 5 words)\n  - Primary keyword in first 100 words (opening hook)\n  - Primary keyword in at least one H2 and the Conclusion\n  - Secondary keywords must appear in H2 headings and their body paragraphs.\n\n## 🛠️ PRE-FLIGHT CHECKLIST\nBefore generating any text, in your `<thinking>` block, confirm:\n- [ ] I have the primary and secondary keywords.\n- [ ] I have the source URLs from the `RESEARCH_JSON` input.\n- [ ] I will write EXACTLY in this order: META → Hook → H2#1 → H2#2 → Conclusion → FAQs → Sources.\n- [ ] I will write NOTHING outside this order.\n\n============================\nARTICLE STRUCTURE (STRICT)\n============================\n\n### SECTION 1 (REQUIRED): META BLOCK\nOutput this exact block at the very top. No conversational filler before it. The very first word must be \"META\".\n- SEO Title: [50–60 characters maximum — MUST follow formula: Primary Keyword + Brand Name + Value]\n- Meta Description: [150–160 characters maximum — MUST follow formula: What the article offers + Unique angle + Call to Action]\n- URL Slug: [lowercase-hyphens-only, e.g. bitcoin-price-hits-new-all-time-high]\n- Categories: [e.g. Bitcoin News, Latest News]\n- Primary Keyword: [e.g. \"Bitcoin price drop\"]\n- Secondary Keywords: [e.g. \"BTC resistance\", \"crypto market pullback\", \"$80K Bitcoin\"]\n\n---\n\n### SECTION 2 (REQUIRED): OPENING HOOK\n**# [H1 Title — must contain primary keyword in first 5 words, include a price or number]**\n\n*(Do NOT add an H2 heading above these paragraphs)*\nPara 1: State the core event with a specific price or data point. 2–3 sentences. Start with the primary keyword naturally. Be direct.\nPara 2: Market reaction or supporting data. One additional observation (volume, altcoins, funding rates). 2–3 sentences.\nPara 3: One-line reframe that sets the article's tone. (\"This isn't panic. It looks like traders taking money off the table after a strong run.\")\n\n---\n\n### SECTION 3 (REQUIRED): FIRST H2 SECTION\n**## [Secondary Keyword 1]: [What It Means / Signals / Shows]**\n\n[1–2 intro paragraphs explaining the news at a high level.]\n[CHART_PLACEHOLDER]\n\n**### [Sub-angle 1: e.g. \"Profit-Taking Shows Up at Resistance\"]**\n[1–2 short paragraphs]\n[Optional 3-item bullet list]\n\n**### [Sub-angle 2: e.g. \"Altcoins Start Losing Steam\"]**\n[2–3 very short paragraphs — 1 to 3 sentences each]\n\n---\n\n### SECTION 4 (REQUIRED): SECOND H2 SECTION\n**## Key [Levels / Data Signals / Numbers] Traders Should Watch [Now / Next]**\n\n[1 intro paragraph]\n[CHART_PLACEHOLDER]\n\n**### [Level/Signal 1: e.g. \"$75K Needs to Hold\"]**\n[1 paragraph — what this level means, what happens if it holds/breaks]\n\n**### [Level/Signal 2: e.g. \"$80K Remains the Barrier\"]**\n[1 paragraph]\n[Required 3-item bullet list starting with \"Look for:\"]\n\n---\n\n### SECTION 5 (REQUIRED): CONCLUSION\n**## Conclusion**\n\nPara 1: Restate the core event in one sentence using the primary keyword. Name the key level or data point.\nPara 2: What it means for the near-term structure. One or two clean sentences.\nPara 3: Actionable close for the reader. What they should do or watch. End on a direct, confident note.\n\n---\n\n### SECTION 6 (REQUIRED): FAQS\n**## FAQs**\n\n**1. Why did [primary event] happen?**\n[3–4 sentence answer. Target ~60 words.]\n\n**2. Is this the start of a bigger [correction/rally/trend]?**\n[3–4 sentence answer. Target ~60 words.]\n\n**3. What [levels/signals] should traders watch now?**\n[3–4 sentence answer. Target ~60 words.]\n\n**4. How does this affect [related asset/market sector]?**\n[3–4 sentence answer. Target ~60 words.]\n\n**5. What is the historical context for this move?**\n[3–4 sentence answer. Target ~60 words.]\n\n**6. What are the short-term risks for investors?**\n[3–4 sentence answer. Target ~60 words.]\n\n---\n\n### SECTION 7 (REQUIRED): SOURCES FOOTER\n**Sources:**\n- [Publication Name]: [source URL from research facts]\n- [Publication Name]: [source URL from research facts]\n\nSTOP GENERATING HERE. DO NOT ADD ANY BROADER CONTEXT SECTIONS. \nState your final word count on the last line of your output as: [Word Count: XXXX]\n\n## 🛡️ POST-OUTPUT VERIFICATION\nBefore finalizing your generation, mentally verify:\n[ ] Exactly 1 H1, 2 H2s, 4 H3s, Conclusion, FAQs, Sources?\n[ ] No em-dashes anywhere?\n[ ] Primary keyword in H1 first 5 words?\n[ ] 2-3 anchor links embedded in body text pointing to source URLs?\n[ ] Word count stated on final line?\n[ ] No sections added beyond the 7 defined above?\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779102598654}}
{"type":"message","id":"348ebcaa","parentId":"bcaf108c","timestamp":"2026-05-18T11:11:11.965Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_PTPgQrEiO5qECnyfPsPwI79t","name":"write","arguments":{"path":"/tmp/crypto-article-raw.md","content":"META\n- SEO Title: Aave recovery: Coinography on WETH borrowing reset\n- Meta Description: See how Aave restored WETH borrowing after the rsETH exploit, what TVL and ETH utilization say next, and which levels traders should track closely now.\n- URL Slug: aave-recovery-restores-weth-borrowing\n- Categories: DeFi News, Latest News\n- Primary Keyword: Aave recovery\n- Secondary Keywords: WETH borrowing, Aave TVL\n\n# Aave recovery pushes $14.8B protocol back into play\n\nAave recovery moved into a new phase after the lending protocol restored WETH borrowing across Ethereum Core, Ethereum Prime, Arbitrum, Base, Mantle and Linea. The change follows the April 18 exploit, when 116,500 rsETH were stolen from Kelp DAO’s LayerZero-powered bridge and later used on Aave V3, leaving about $195 million in bad debt.\n\nThe reopening comes with softer money-market conditions. Aave TVL sits near $14.8 billion, still well below the $23.5 billion seen in March, while ETH utilisation has dropped under 90% and borrow APY has fallen to 1.9%. Lower borrowing costs can bring leveraged Ether yield trades back into view if confidence keeps rebuilding.\n\nThis is not a full reset. It is a test of whether liquidity returns faster than risk appetite fades.\n\n## WETH borrowing: What Aave recovery shows\n\nAccording to [Cointelegraph](https://cointelegraph.com/news/aave-restores-weth-borrowing-after-kelp-dao-rseth-exploit?utm_source=rss_feed&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss_partner_inbound), users can once again borrow against WETH and execute collateral or debt swaps after the protocol restored pre-incident loan-to-value ratios. That move closes the final step of Phase II in the rsETH recovery plan.\n\n[CoinDesk reported](https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2026/05/18/aave-restores-weth-collateral-limits-as-rseth-crisis-enters-recovery-phase) that the rollback covers six networks and reverses restrictions imposed after April’s exploit as contagion fears eased. Aave had frozen WETH as a safety measure while rsETH and wrsETH reserves were also halted.\n\n[CHART_PLACEHOLDER]\n\n### Liquidity Conditions Have Improved\n\nThe reopening only makes sense if the balance sheet is less stressed. Tom Wan said ETH utilisation is back below 90%, which means the system has more idle Ether available to absorb fresh borrowing demand without immediately squeezing rates higher.\n\nAt the same time, borrow APY near 1.9% lowers the cost of levering back into ETH yield loops. For active traders, that matters more than the governance headline because cheap financing can quickly pull dormant demand back into the market.\n\n- WETH LTVs are back to pre-incident levels across the affected markets.\n- Ether liquidity is less constrained than it was in the first weeks after the hack.\n- Borrow costs are low enough to make loop strategies worth reviewing again.\n\n### Depositors Are Still Cautious\n\nThe balance sheet has not fully healed. Wan said wstETH deposits are down $1.2 billion since the hack, and weETH deposits are down $1.76 billion.\n\nThat pullback matters because Aave recovery still depends on sticky collateral returning, not just short-term borrowers showing up for cheaper financing. If deposit growth lags while borrow demand rebounds, the protocol could move from comfortable liquidity back to a tighter setup quickly.\n\nKelp DAO is also still adjusting its own structure. The protocol said it will sunset rsETH bridging on several networks after June 15, which keeps operational risk in focus even as Aave restores normal borrowing functions.\n\n## Key Aave TVL data signals traders should watch now\n\nThe cleanest scoreboard is still Aave TVL. It stands near $14.8 billion, far below the $23.5 billion seen in March, so [the recovery progress outlined by Cointelegraph](https://cointelegraph.com/news/aave-restores-weth-borrowing-after-kelp-dao-rseth-exploit?utm_source=rss_feed&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss_partner_inbound) is not yet the same as a full capital recovery.\n\n[CHART_PLACEHOLDER]\n\n### $14.8B TVL Needs to Stabilize\n\nIf TVL can hold or grind higher while WETH borrowing resumes, traders can read that as capital staying in the system instead of leaving on the first sign of restored access. A renewed slide would suggest the protocol fixed a function but not confidence, and that would keep the recovery fragile.\n\n### 90% ETH Utilisation Remains the Trigger\n\nUtilisation below 90% and borrow APY at 1.9% are the numbers to watch together. If utilisation climbs fast as loop demand returns, rates can rise and pressure new positions. If utilisation stays contained, Aave recovery has room to extend without another scramble for Ether liquidity.\n\n- Look for: ETH utilisation holding below 90% as borrowing volume returns.\n- Borrow APY staying near current levels instead of snapping higher.\n- WETH and liquid staking deposits rebuilding after the April outflows.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nAave recovery now has a clear marker: WETH borrowing is live again, and the protocol is operating from a $14.8 billion TVL base instead of outright crisis mode.\n\nNear-term structure depends less on the reopen itself and more on whether deposits return while utilisation stays controlled. That will tell traders if this is repair with staying power or only a temporary easing of stress.\n\nWatch TVL, ETH utilisation and borrow APY together. If those three stay in line, the reopening has substance. If they deteriorate, treat this as a bounce in confidence, not a full return.\n\n## FAQs\n\n**1. Why did Aave recovery happen?**\n\nAave recovery happened because the protocol moved forward with the rsETH technical recovery plan after earlier emergency restrictions. Governance approved lifting the WETH freeze once recovery steps had restored more protection for users. That included progress on rsETH backing and the use of frozen Ether plus donated tokens to support the broader repair effort.\n\n**2. Is this the start of a bigger trend?**\n\nIt could be the start of a steadier normalization phase, but the data does not confirm a full comeback yet. TVL is still far below March levels, which means capital has not fully returned. The stronger signal is that ETH utilisation is lower and borrow APY is cheaper, giving the market room to re-engage if confidence continues to improve.\n\n**3. What levels and signals should traders watch now?**\n\nThe main numbers are $14.8 billion in TVL, sub-90% ETH utilisation and borrow APY near 1.9%. Traders should also monitor whether wstETH and weETH deposits keep shrinking or begin to rebuild. A recovery with stable liquidity is more durable than one driven only by short-term borrowers chasing cheap leverage.\n\n**4. How does this affect Ether and liquid staking markets?**\n\nLower borrow costs can make Ether loop strategies more attractive again, which may lift activity tied to WETH, wstETH and weETH. At the same time, the decline in liquid staking deposits shows that users are still selective with risk. If those deposits recover, the signal for Ether-linked DeFi positioning becomes much stronger.\n\n**5. What is the historical context for this move?**\n\nThe backdrop is the April 18 exploit tied to Kelp DAO’s LayerZero-powered bridge, where 116,500 rsETH were stolen and then used on Aave V3. The incident created roughly $195 million in bad debt on Aave and knocked more than $8 billion off TVL. This reopening is the first real sign that emergency restrictions are being rolled back in size.\n\n**6. What are the short-term risks for investors?**\n\nThe biggest risk is that borrowing demand returns faster than collateral and liquidity do. That would push utilisation higher, raise rates and make the reopen look less stable. Investors should also keep an eye on Kelp DAO’s network consolidation and the possibility that capital stays on the sidelines or rotates to rival lending venues instead.\n\n**Sources:**\n- CoinDesk: https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2026/05/18/aave-restores-weth-collateral-limits-as-rseth-crisis-enters-recovery-phase\n- CoinTelegraph: https://cointelegraph.com/news/aave-restores-weth-borrowing-after-kelp-dao-rseth-exploit?utm_source=rss_feed&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss_partner_inbound\n\n[Word Count: 0]"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"local-bifrost","model":"gpt-5.4","usage":{"input":1570,"output":5726,"cacheRead":8192,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":15488,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1779102598655,"responseId":"chatcmpl-Dgq9CaKVwQhp4QoEJp9TJkm1u6PTd"}}
