{"type":"message","id":"95b3081a","parentId":"ff86a88f","timestamp":"2026-05-18T11:11:42.806Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_DgAy6tbMJKU0kTXuRYbtGkLo","toolName":"edit","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Successfully replaced 14 block(s) in /tmp/crypto-article-raw.md."}],"details":{"diff":"    ...\n  9 # Aave recovery pushes $14.8B protocol back into play\n 10 \n 11 Aave recovery moved into a new phase after the lending protocol restored WETH borrowing across Ethereum Core, Ethereum Prime, Arbitrum, Base, Mantle and Linea. The change follows the April 18 exploit, when 116,500 rsETH were stolen from Kelp DAO’s LayerZero-powered bridge and later used on Aave V3, leaving about $195 million in bad debt.\n 12 \n-13 The reopening comes with softer money-market conditions. Aave TVL sits near $14.8 billion, still well below the $23.5 billion seen in March, while ETH utilisation has dropped under 90% and borrow APY has fallen to 1.9%. Lower borrowing costs can bring leveraged Ether yield trades back into view if confidence keeps rebuilding.\n+13 The reopening comes with softer money-market conditions. Aave TVL sits near $14.8 billion, still well below the $23.5 billion seen in March, while ETH utilisation has dropped under 90% and borrow APY has fallen to 1.9%.\n 14 \n 15 This is not a full reset. It is a test of whether liquidity returns faster than risk appetite fades.\n 16 \n 17 ## WETH borrowing: What Aave recovery shows\n    ...\n 25 ### Liquidity Conditions Have Improved\n 26 \n 27 The reopening only makes sense if the balance sheet is less stressed. Tom Wan said ETH utilisation is back below 90%, which means the system has more idle Ether available to absorb fresh borrowing demand without immediately squeezing rates higher.\n 28 \n-29 At the same time, borrow APY near 1.9% lowers the cost of levering back into ETH yield loops. For active traders, that matters more than the governance headline because cheap financing can quickly pull dormant demand back into the market.\n+29 At the same time, borrow APY near 1.9% lowers the cost of levering back into ETH yield loops. For active traders, cheap financing can quickly pull dormant demand back into the market.\n 30 \n 31 - WETH LTVs are back to pre-incident levels across the affected markets.\n 32 - Ether liquidity is less constrained than it was in the first weeks after the hack.\n 33 - Borrow costs are low enough to make loop strategies worth reviewing again.\n    ...\n 35 ### Depositors Are Still Cautious\n 36 \n 37 The balance sheet has not fully healed. Wan said wstETH deposits are down $1.2 billion since the hack, and weETH deposits are down $1.76 billion.\n 38 \n-39 That pullback matters because Aave recovery still depends on sticky collateral returning, not just short-term borrowers showing up for cheaper financing. If deposit growth lags while borrow demand rebounds, the protocol could move from comfortable liquidity back to a tighter setup quickly.\n+39 That pullback matters because Aave recovery still depends on collateral returning, not just short-term borrowers showing up for cheaper financing. If deposit growth lags while borrow demand rebounds, the protocol could move back to a tighter setup.\n 40 \n-41 Kelp DAO is also still adjusting its own structure. The protocol said it will sunset rsETH bridging on several networks after June 15, which keeps operational risk in focus even as Aave restores normal borrowing functions.\n+41 Kelp DAO is also still adjusting its own structure. The protocol said it will sunset rsETH bridging on several networks after June 15, which keeps operational risk in focus.\n 42 \n 43 ## Key Aave TVL data signals traders should watch now\n 44 \n 45 The cleanest scoreboard is still Aave TVL. It stands near $14.8 billion, far below the $23.5 billion seen in March, so [the recovery progress outlined by Cointelegraph](https://cointelegraph.com/news/aave-restores-weth-borrowing-after-kelp-dao-rseth-exploit?utm_source=rss_feed&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss_partner_inbound) is not yet the same as a full capital recovery.\n    ...\n 47 [CHART_PLACEHOLDER]\n 48 \n 49 ### $14.8B TVL Needs to Stabilize\n 50 \n-51 If TVL can hold or grind higher while WETH borrowing resumes, traders can read that as capital staying in the system instead of leaving on the first sign of restored access. A renewed slide would suggest the protocol fixed a function but not confidence, and that would keep the recovery fragile.\n+51 If TVL can hold or grind higher while WETH borrowing resumes, traders can read that as capital staying in the system. A renewed slide would suggest the protocol fixed a function but not confidence.\n 52 \n 53 ### 90% ETH Utilisation Remains the Trigger\n 54 \n-55 Utilisation below 90% and borrow APY at 1.9% are the numbers to watch together. If utilisation climbs fast as loop demand returns, rates can rise and pressure new positions. If utilisation stays contained, Aave recovery has room to extend without another scramble for Ether liquidity.\n+55 Utilisation below 90% and borrow APY at 1.9% are the numbers to watch together. If utilisation climbs fast as loop demand returns, rates can rise and pressure new positions. If utilisation stays contained, Aave recovery has room to extend.\n 56 \n 57 - Look for: ETH utilisation holding below 90% as borrowing volume returns.\n 58 - Borrow APY staying near current levels instead of snapping higher.\n 59 - WETH and liquid staking deposits rebuilding after the April outflows.\n    ...\n 61 ## Conclusion\n 62 \n 63 Aave recovery now has a clear marker: WETH borrowing is live again, and the protocol is operating from a $14.8 billion TVL base instead of outright crisis mode.\n 64 \n-65 Near-term structure depends less on the reopen itself and more on whether deposits return while utilisation stays controlled. That will tell traders if this is repair with staying power or only a temporary easing of stress.\n+65 Near-term structure depends on whether deposits return while utilisation stays controlled. That will tell traders if this is repair with staying power.\n 66 \n-67 Watch TVL, ETH utilisation and borrow APY together. If those three stay in line, the reopening has substance. If they deteriorate, treat this as a bounce in confidence, not a full return.\n+67 Watch TVL, ETH utilisation and borrow APY together. If those three stay in line, the reopening has substance. If they deteriorate, treat this as a bounce in confidence.\n 68 \n 69 ## FAQs\n 70 \n 71 **1. Why did Aave recovery happen?**\n 72 \n-73 Aave recovery happened because the protocol moved forward with the rsETH technical recovery plan after earlier emergency restrictions. Governance approved lifting the WETH freeze once recovery steps had restored more protection for users. That included progress on rsETH backing and the use of frozen Ether plus donated tokens to support the broader repair effort.\n+73 Aave recovery happened because the protocol moved forward with the rsETH technical recovery plan after earlier emergency restrictions. Governance approved lifting the WETH freeze once recovery steps restored more protection for users. That included progress on rsETH backing and the use of frozen Ether plus donated tokens.\n 74 \n 75 **2. Is this the start of a bigger trend?**\n 76 \n-77 It could be the start of a steadier normalization phase, but the data does not confirm a full comeback yet. TVL is still far below March levels, which means capital has not fully returned. The stronger signal is that ETH utilisation is lower and borrow APY is cheaper, giving the market room to re-engage if confidence continues to improve.\n+77 It could be the start of a steadier normalization phase, but the data does not confirm a full comeback yet. TVL is still far below March levels, which means capital has not fully returned. The stronger signal is that ETH utilisation is lower and borrow APY is cheaper.\n 78 \n 79 **3. What levels and signals should traders watch now?**\n 80 \n-81 The main numbers are $14.8 billion in TVL, sub-90% ETH utilisation and borrow APY near 1.9%. Traders should also monitor whether wstETH and weETH deposits keep shrinking or begin to rebuild. A recovery with stable liquidity is more durable than one driven only by short-term borrowers chasing cheap leverage.\n+81 The main numbers are $14.8 billion in TVL, sub-90% ETH utilisation and borrow APY near 1.9%. Traders should also monitor whether wstETH and weETH deposits keep shrinking or begin to rebuild. A recovery with stable liquidity is stronger than one driven only by short-term borrowers.\n 82 \n 83 **4. How does this affect Ether and liquid staking markets?**\n 84 \n-85 Lower borrow costs can make Ether loop strategies more attractive again, which may lift activity tied to WETH, wstETH and weETH. At the same time, the decline in liquid staking deposits shows that users are still selective with risk. If those deposits recover, the signal for Ether-linked DeFi positioning becomes much stronger.\n+85 Lower borrow costs can make Ether loop strategies more attractive again, which may lift activity tied to WETH, wstETH and weETH. The decline in liquid staking deposits shows that users are still selective with risk. If those deposits recover, the signal for Ether-linked DeFi positioning becomes much stronger.\n 86 \n 87 **5. What is the historical context for this move?**\n 88 \n-89 The backdrop is the April 18 exploit tied to Kelp DAO’s LayerZero-powered bridge, where 116,500 rsETH were stolen and then used on Aave V3. The incident created roughly $195 million in bad debt on Aave and knocked more than $8 billion off TVL. This reopening is the first real sign that emergency restrictions are being rolled back in size.\n+89 The backdrop is the April 18 exploit tied to Kelp DAO’s LayerZero-powered bridge, where 116,500 rsETH were stolen and then used on Aave V3. The incident created roughly $195 million in bad debt on Aave and knocked more than $8 billion off TVL. This reopening is the clearest sign that emergency restrictions are being rolled back.\n 90 \n 91 **6. What are the short-term risks for investors?**\n 92 \n-93 The biggest risk is that borrowing demand returns faster than collateral and liquidity do. That would push utilisation higher, raise rates and make the reopen look less stable. Investors should also keep an eye on Kelp DAO’s network consolidation and the possibility that capital stays on the sidelines or rotates to rival lending venues instead.\n+93 The biggest risk is that borrowing demand returns faster than collateral and liquidity do. That would push utilisation higher, raise rates and make the reopen look less stable. Investors should also watch Kelp DAO’s network consolidation and the chance that capital rotates to rival lending venues.\n 94 \n 95 **Sources:**\n 96 - CoinDesk: https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2026/05/18/aave-restores-weth-collateral-limits-as-rseth-crisis-enters-recovery-phase\n 97 - CoinTelegraph: https://cointelegraph.com/news/aave-restores-weth-borrowing-after-kelp-dao-rseth-exploit?utm_source=rss_feed&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss_partner_inbound\n    ...","firstChangedLine":13},"isError":false,"timestamp":1779102702806}}
